## The Problem: Tool Testing Dilemma

Most coding agent tools directly call `std::fs::read` or `Command::new`. This makes testing difficult:
- Tests need a real filesystem
- Tests might accidentally modify real files
- Tests interfere with each other
- CI environments might lack certain commands

ki's solution is to **abstract environment operations**.

## ExecutionEnv trait

`ExecutionEnv` defines the interface for all environment operations:

```rust
pub trait ExecutionEnv: Send + Sync + 'static {
    // Filesystem operations
    fn read_file(&self, path: &Path) -> Result<FileContent, FileError>;
    fn write_file(&self, path: &Path, content: &str) -> Result<(), FileError>;
    fn file_info(&self, path: &Path) -> Result<FileInfo, FileError>;

    // Shell operations
    fn execute_shell(&self, command: &str, options: ShellExecOptions) -> Result<ShellOutput, ExecutionError>;

    // Path operations
    fn current_dir(&self) -> Result<PathBuf, ExecutionError>;
}
```

Two implementations:

```rust
// Production: real OS
pub struct OsExecutionEnv {
    cwd: PathBuf,
}

// Testing: in-memory
pub struct InMemoryExecutionEnv {
    files: Arc<RwLock<HashMap<PathBuf, String>>>,
    cwd: PathBuf,
}
```

## How Tools Use ExecutionEnv

Tools don't directly operate on the environment; they hold `ExecutionEnv` through `ExecutionToolContext`:

```rust
pub struct ExecutionToolContext {
    env: Arc<dyn ExecutionEnv>,
    mutation_queue: Arc<MutationQueue>,
}

// Pass context when creating tools
pub fn create_read_tool(ctx: &ExecutionToolContext) -> Box<dyn AgentTool> {
    Box::new(ReadTool {
        env: ctx.env.clone(),
    })
}

// Tool implementation
impl AgentTool for ReadTool {
    async fn execute(&self, params: ToolCallParams) -> AgentToolResult {
        let path = parse_path(&params.arguments)?;
        match self.env.read_file(&path) {
            Ok(content) => AgentToolResult::success(content.text),
            Err(e) => AgentToolResult::error(format!("Read failed: {}", e)),
        }
    }
}
```

